---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import CaseStudyBlock from '../../components/CaseStudyBlock.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Increasing B2B Traffic: One Confusing Toggle | sivani.me"
	description="Updated our design system to fit new B2B brand guidelines, and discovered a critical issue that was bottlenecking the number of users that landed on our B2B pages."
>
	<SiteHeader />
	<div class="content">
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<a class="back-link" href="/">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 17 17" width="17" height="17" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
						<path
							d="M9.1 3.7 L4.3 8.5 L9.1 13.3"
							stroke="currentColor"
							stroke-width="1"
							stroke-linecap="round"
							stroke-linejoin="round"
							fill="none"
						/>
						<path d="M4.3 8.5 H13.7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" fill="none" />
					</svg>
					<span>Home</span>
				</a>
				<div class="hero__body">
					<div class="hero__heading">
						<h1>Increasing B2B Traffic: One Confusing Toggle</h1>
						<p class="hero__company">Company: Code Chrysalis</p>
					</div>
					<div class="hero__image">
						<img src="/images/case-study-3/cover.svg" alt="" />
					</div>
					<hr />
					<p>
						Updated our design system to fit new B2B brand guidelines, and discovered a critical issue that was
						bottlenecking the number of users that landed on our B2B pages.
					</p>
					<hr />
					<p class="hero__skills">
						Skills: stakeholder management, design systems, brand implementation, navigation design, ui design,
						analytics review, project management
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Summary" />

			<section class="summary">
				<h2>Challenge</h2>
				<p>
					Our company was looking to expand the scope of its B2B offerings, and target a wider range of clients,
					including SMBs (small and medium-sized businesses). Thus far, the B2B side of our site – that existed on
					the same domain as our B2C site – had gotten little traffic, much less compared to the B2C side. Given
					our changing business goals, we were looking to increase the number of users landing on our B2B pages,
					redesign our existing B2B pages, and add more content explaining our newly expanded B2B offerings.
				</p>

				<h2>Key insight</h2>
				<p>
					Evaluating the B2C side of the site ended up surfacing a critical B2B issue: users didn't always know
					the B2B side even existed. Our B2C site was the default homepage and outranked B2B in search, and had
					been around longer since B2B programs were added later. A toggle existed on the navbar to switch
					between B2C and B2B – but this toggle conflated language with business/audience type. Switching from
					English to Japanese switched from B2C to B2B – but this was an internal understanding within our
					company that did not make intuitive sense to our users.
				</p>
				<p>
					The highest impact solution to this problem ended up being surprisingly simple, in contrast to the
					thorniness of the problem it solved: what I needed to do was fix a button.
				</p>

				<h2>Outcomes</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						I removed the language toggle entirely, replacing it with navbar links that explicitly stated where
						they would take the user, depending on whether they were on the B2C or B2B side of the site.
					</li>
					<li>
						After making this change, we tracked our traffic via our analytics dashboard and confirmed that we had
						higher traffic heading to our B2B pages in the months following this change. We also saw a higher
						average scroll percentage, and higher average number of clicks on the redesigned Home and Corporate
						Training pages.
					</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Deep dive" />

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-3/context.svg" title="Context">
				<p>
					Soon after joining the Product team, Code Chrysalis's external website was handed over to me to manage
					– it had previously been managed by the marketing teams, and updated by contractors.
				</p>
				<p>
					Our company was looking to expand the scope of its B2B offerings, and target a wider range of clients,
					including SMBs (small and medium-sized businesses). Their goal was to add new content to the site,
					explaining their programs in detail, in order to better leverage the site as material to promote their
					services. Thus far, the B2B side of our site – that existed on the same domain as our B2C site – had
					gotten little traffic, much less compared to the B2C side. Given our changing business goals, we were
					looking to increase the number of users landing on our B2B pages. To do that, we needed to better
					understand why this gap in traffic existed.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-3/my-role.svg" title="Your Role">
				<p>
					I was the sole designer on this project, and owned the entire process end-to-end: I conducted research,
					identified problems, designed and implemented solutions, and measured their impact. I managed this
					project as a cross-team collaboration between the Product and B2B teams.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-3/process.svg" title="Process">
				<p>
					Held weekly syncs with the B2B Marketing Manager, since this was a long-term project with many large
					content updates to the B2B side of the site, of which the copy and media were supplied to me by the
					marketing team.
				</p>
				<p>Received new brand guidelines and implemented them into the design system.</p>
				<p>Redesigned existing pages (Home and Corporate Training) to fit the new design system.</p>
				<p>Created design and structure for new offering pages and landing pages.</p>
				<p>Identified updates to make to site navigation.</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-3/key-insights.svg" title="Key Insight">
				<p>
					A related evaluation of this site's B2C side surfaced a discoverability problem that turned out to
					affect B2B just as much: our B2C site was the default homepage and outranked B2B in search, and had been
					around longer since B2B programs were added later. This meant many users ended up on the B2C site, and:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Did not know our company had a B2B department.</li>
					<li>Did not know how to find information about our B2B offerings, even once they were on our homepage.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					This was because in the past, language had been conflated with business/audience type. Our B2C pages
					were all in English. A language toggle existed via clicking on a Japanese flag. Clicking on this flag
					would switch the site to Japanese – and also switch to the B2B side of the site. There were multiple
					issues with this:
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li>The Japanese flag looked like it signaled "current state" when displayed on the English pages – this was confusing.</li>
					<li>
						We were assuming that an internal assumption within our company would be understood by external
						clients. Internally, we understood that B2C bootcamps primarily served English-speaking customers and
						B2B programs primarily served Japanese-speaking customers. A user visiting our site would not know
						this.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					The highest impact solution to this problem ended up being surprisingly simple, in contrast to the
					thorniness of the problem it solved: what I needed to do was fix a button.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-3/outcomes.svg" title="Outcome">
				<p>
					To make sure it was easy for a user entering our more discoverable B2C site to find their way to the
					B2B side, I had to redesign the toggle. I designed and implemented this change myself (Figma for
					design, Webflow for development).
				</p>
				<p>This involved:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						Communication with the B2C and B2B teams to confirm that localizing content was not a priority – we
						were happy keeping B2C all in English, and B2B all in Japanese
					</li>
					<li>Removing the language toggle entirely in favor of a B2B/B2C toggle</li>
					<li>
						Identifying the best copy to make this toggle clear – I ended up adding a navbar link with Japanese
						copy and an English tooltip on hover to the B2C site, and added a similar navbar link on the B2B site
						(English copy, Japanese tooltip on hover). This prioritized visibility in each audience's primary
						language, while still allowing other users to understand the meaning of the toggle via hover.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					In the following months, I tracked traffic via our analytics dashboard and confirmed that we now had
					higher traffic heading to our B2B pages. Moreover, after attending the DXPO, the B2B Marketing Manager
					and I reviewed our site data and confirmed a higher average scroll percentage, and higher average number
					of clicks on the redesigned Home and Corporate Training pages.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<div class="cta-wrap">
				<a class="cta-button" href="/">&larr; Home</a>
			</div>
		</main>
	</div>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
	}

	.back-link {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-micro);
		width: fit-content;
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		transition: color 0.2s ease;
	}

	.back-link:hover {
		color: var(--color-medium-green);
	}

	.hero__body {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.hero__heading {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
	}

	.hero__heading h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h1);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.hero__company {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.hero__image {
		aspect-ratio: 2000 / 1125;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 5px;
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.hero__image img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
	}

	.hero__body hr {
		width: 100%;
		height: 1px;
		margin: 0;
		border: none;
		background-color: var(--color-light-grey);
		border-radius: 5px;
	}

	.hero__body p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.hero__skills {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.summary {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.summary h2 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h2);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.summary p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.summary ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.cta-wrap {
		padding: 0 var(--space-padding-large) var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		height: 33px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 18px;
		background-color: var(--color-light-purple);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		transition: filter 0.2s ease;
	}

	.cta-button:hover {
		filter: brightness(0.93);
	}
</style>
